1. c4 e5 2. Nc3 Nf6 3. Nf3 Nc6 4. d4 exd4 5. Nxd4 d5 6. cxd5 Nxd5 7. Nxc6 bxc6
8. e4 Nxc3 9. Qxd8+ Kxd8 10. bxc3 Bd6 11. Be3 Re8 12. Bd3 Bd7 13. O-O c5 14.
Rfd1 Re7 15. Rab1 Ke8 16. f3 Kf8 17. Bb5 Rd8 18. Bxd7 Rexd7 19. Rd5 Ke8 20.
Bxc5 Bxc5+ 21. Rxc5 Rd1+ 22. Rxd1 Rxd1+ 23. Kf2 Rd2+ 24. Kg3 Rxa2 25. Rxc7 a5
26. Ra7 a4 27. c4 a3 28. h4 Ra1 29. Kf4 a2 30. g4 Kd8 31. g5 Kc8 32. h5 Kb8 33.
Ra4 Kc7 34. Ke5 Kd7 35. f4 Ke7 36. Ra7+ Kd8 37. h6 gxh6 38. gxh6 Rh1 39. Rxa2
Rxh6 40. f5 Rc6 41. Kd5 Rc7 42. Ra8+ Ke7 43. Rh8 Rd7+ 44. Kc5 Kf6 45. Rxh7 Ke5
46. Rh4 Rc7+ 47. Kb5 f6 48. c5 Rxc5+ {Sources: (whole game) Tinsley notebooks;
(finish) Manchester Guardian, 2 Aug 1934; BCM, Sept 1934, p363; BCM, July 1988,
p294. Charles Warburton's book 'My Chess Adventures' describes what happened
next. "Abrahams, in his excitement, let out an exultant whoop and rising
noisily from his seat danced merrily round the neighbouring tables to the
consternation of many players and the horror of the stewards. Play was stopped
as were clocks until after several minutes calm and peace were restored and
ruffled feathers reset. All this to the great glee of the spectators." (Quoted
in 'A History of Lancashire Chess' (2009) by MJ Conroy, p193} 1/2-1/2

