{Unfortunately, Tinsley makes a terrible hash of the notation of this game.
It's 'good in parts' but goes completely haywire twice - JS...} 1. d4 e6 2. Nf3
Nf6 3. c4 Bb4+ 4. Bd2 Qe7 5. g3 O-O 6. Bg2 d5 7. Qc2 Nc6 8. a3 Bxd2+ {Here
Tinsley's notation is "8...BxKt 9.QPxB" which makes no sense at all. I have
assumed the sequence as shown, after which the notation settles down and looks
correct...} 9. Nbxd2 e5 10. dxe5 Nxe5 11. Nxe5 Qxe5 12. cxd5 Nxd5 13. O-O Nf6
14. e4 Be6 15. Rad1 Rad8 16. Kh1 c6 17. f4 Qc7 18. h3 Nh5 19. Kh2 g5 {... now
the spaces for White and Black's 20th moves are left blank on the page, and
Tinsley proceeds with "21.PxP" which seems highly improbable. So we are
looking for two missing half-moves, probably here but possibly earlier...} 20.
e5 gxf4 {and now we can make some sense of the moves written, thus...} 21. gxf4
f6 22. Ne4 fxe5 23. Rxd8 {"RxRch" but it's not check} Rxd8 24. f5 Bd5 25. f6
Rf8 26. Qf2 Bxe4 27. Bxe4 Nxf6 28. Qg3+ Kh8 29. Qh4 Qg7 (29... Ng4+ {wins but
I suppose it is feasible that Black missed this tactic in time trouble - JS})
30. Rg1 Qf7 31. Rf1 {Tinsley's notation here looks like "QKBsq" but must
presumbly be the text...} Qg7 {"Drawn by repetition" Sources: Tinsley
notebooks. I'm not entirely confident that I have reconstructed correctly but
this could be right. It seems a bit unlikely that Black should have abandoned
his winning attempts but he may have been in time trouble - JS} 1/2-1/2

